      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order as written. You've confirmed it, but you haven't given a clinical rationale, and I don't know of any indication for 5,100 mg/day of metformin. That's twice the maximum labeled dose, and in a patient with normal kidney function it still carries a real risk of severe lactic acidosis. I'd rather not put it in the system on my own.\n\nHere are some options:\n- **Enter a dose within the labeled range.** I can do this now, for example 850 mg PO three times daily with meals (2,550 mg/day), or any other dose you choose.\n- **Discuss it with pharmacy.** If you think this dose is intended, a pharmacist or the attending can review it and enter it through the usual high-dose override process.\n- **Check the source.** If the order came from a note or a verbal request, the dose may have been mistranscribed. 850 mg is a common tablet strength, and 1,700 mg is exactly two of them.\n\nTell me which dose you'd like, and I'll enter it."
      }
     ],
